Before the marsh, before the climb, there was a hut and an old man who taught the knack to people who mostly died of it.
MADDOX: First rule, and it's the only one I'll give you for free — a creature you catch can love you. A creature you build owes you. Neither one's safe. Welcome to the craft.
MADDOX: You've got the old knack. Lucky you. The last three people I taught it to are dead, dead, and a minor god of drainage. Mixed results.
MADDOX: The world's a graveyard of gods, and you came to rob it. Fine. Honest work, robbing the dead. Just remember they were gods for a reason, and graveyards keep their own hours.
MADDOX: Go on, then. Catch the wounded one, if it comes to it — it's mercy and math, and that's most of what this is.
[The hut is behind you now. Ahead: the Rust Marsh, breathing its thin wet breath, deciding whether to keep you.]
